<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Lament · c. 1937 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Umi Yukaba</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">海ゆかば</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Umi Yukaba</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese war song made in 1937 from an old poem. It was played on the radio during the Second World War when soldiers died.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ōtomo no Yakamochi (from his long poem in the Man'yōshū, poem 4094)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Kiyoshi Nobutoki (1937); an earlier setting by Tōgi Sueyoshi (1880)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Japan; the Imperial Japanese Navy</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese (Old Japanese of the Man'yōshū)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words c. 749; earlier setting 1880; Nobutoki's melody 1937</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words from a chōka by Ōtomo no Yakamochi in the Man'yōshū (8th century); melody by Kiyoshi Nobutoki, 1937)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The words of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a>&quot; come from a chōka, a long poem, by Ōtomo no Yakamochi in the Man'yōshū, the 8th-century anthology of Japanese poetry (poem 4094; book 18). The poem celebrates the imperial edict on the discovery of gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Michinoku</a> Province (present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku</a>) in 749. The distant ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-clan">Ōtomo clan</a> were known as masters of the royal Kume guard, and the passage reflects their pledge to serve their sovereign; the part later set to music corresponds closely to the words the edict itself quotes, as recorded in the Shoku <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihongi</a>. Two endings of the passage survive, one from the poem and one from the edict, and the scholar of the Man'yōshū Nakanishi Susumu suggested that the Ōtomo family may have sung both in answer to each other.</p><p>In 1880 Tōgi Sueyoshi, a musician of the Imperial Household Ministry, set the words under the title &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a>&quot; (海行かば); his setting can be heard in the trio of the &quot;Warship March&quot; (Gunkan kōshinkyoku), and before the war it served as the salute for general officers. The best-known melody is Kiyoshi Nobutoki's of 1937, commissioned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> Broadcasting Corporation as the theme of the National Spiritual Mobilization Emphasis Week that the government had declared; it was broadcast from 13 to 16 October 1937. The song became popular in the armed forces, especially the Imperial Japanese Navy, and was widely sung during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, often by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a> pilots before take-off.</p><p>What fixed the song in people's minds was its use on the radio in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-general-headquarters">Imperial General Headquarters</a> announcements that reported the annihilation of units fighting to the last always opened with it, though it was also played for the success of the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>; other victories were announced with songs such as &quot;Battōtai&quot; and the &quot;Warship March&quot;. It was also sung to send soldiers off and to receive the ashes of the dead, which is why some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> regard it as a military song and others as a requiem. In the war years the religious leader Taniguchi Masaharu opposed it, which led to trouble between lecturers of his movement, Seichō-no-Ie, and the Special Higher Police. Yasujirō Ozu used it in the last scene of his film &quot;There Was a Father&quot; (1942); the occupation's censors removed the sound after the war, and the original is known from a print the Soviet army took from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in 1945 the Allied occupation banned &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a>&quot; with other military songs (gunka). After the occupation it came to be widely played again in military circles, including by the Japan Maritime Self-Defense Force. Its words are used in the Yasukuni no mai, a kagura dance of modern making, and Ōbirin school used its melody for its school song until 1958. Nobutoki's published song collections long left it out, a silence the musician Aikawa Yumi criticised in her writings and the notes to her recordings. The quarrel over its character continues: in 2018 the education board of Yachiyo, Chiba Prefecture, at first withdrew its support from a concert of Japanese songs because the programme included what it called a military song, and in September 2025 the Takarazuka Revue dropped a solo of it from a show after criticism that a military song was unsuitable.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A passage from an 8th-century poem expressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-clan">Ōtomo clan</a>'s pledge to serve their sovereign; solemn. In the 1930s and 1940s it was used to send soldiers off and to mourn the dead, and it is remembered both as a war song and as a requiem.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Japanese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">海行かば 水漬く屍</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Umi yukaba mizuku kabane</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If [I] go by sea, a corpse soaked in the water;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">山行かば 草生す屍</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">yama yukaba kusa musu kabane</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if [I] go by the mountains, a corpse with grass growing on it:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">大君の 辺にこそ死なめ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Ōkimi no he ni koso shiname</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at the side of the Great Lord, there indeed let [me] die;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">かへりみはせじ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kaerimi wa seji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[I] will not look back.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ōtomo no Yakamochi, Man'yōshū, book 18 (8th century). Text from <a href="https://ja.wikisource.org/wiki/%E6%B5%B7%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%8B%E3%81%B0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (modified Hepburn).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: modified Hepburn of the words as they are sung, not a letter-by-letter transliteration of the historical kana spelling: かへりみはせじ is sung kaerimi wa seji (へ as e, the particle は as wa). The two-part lines keep the original's space as a plain space.</li><li>The subject is not expressed in the Japanese; [I] / [me] are supplied. It may equally be read as 'we'.</li><li>line 3: 大君 (Ōkimi), 'the Great Lord', is the sovereign, the emperor; 辺 (he) is 'side, vicinity'. The form shiname (死なめ, after koso) is a resolve or wish: 'let me die', 'I shall die'.</li><li>line 4: kaerimi (顧み) is 'looking back'; the line can also be read as 'I will have no regrets' or 'I will not look back to myself'. The sense is disputed only in emphasis.</li><li>line 1: mizuku (水漬く) 'soaked in water, sunk in water'; line 2: kusa musu (草生す) 'grass grows (on it)'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="WxxyzMw-cwM" data-title="Umi Yukaba: the Tokyo Philharmonic Chorus"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">the Tokyo Philharmonic Chorus</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Tokyo Philharmonic Chorus - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WxxyzMw-cwM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Warship March (Gunkan kōshinkyoku), trio</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (Tōgi Sueyoshi's 1880 setting)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hata wa utau (Hayashi Hikaru)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1987</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody, written from an anti-militarist position</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kekkon tsūchi (Kyu Sakamoto)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Yuimei omoshi (anthem of Fuji Taisekiji Kenshōkai)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as (arrangement of the song)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ōbirin school song</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">until 1958</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Yasukuni no mai (kagura)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words used</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">There Was a Father (Chichi ariki, film by Yasujirō Ozu)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1942</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in the last scene</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tora! Tora! Tora! (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">featured in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nihonkai daikaisen: Umi yukaba (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1983</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the song</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ōtomo no Yakamochi, chōka celebrating the edict on the gold of Michinoku, Man'yōshū book 18, poem 4094</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 749</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Edict on the discovery of gold in Michinoku, in the Shoku Nihongi (13th edict)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">749</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words it quotes; source of the second ending</span></li></ul></section>
